//// A `pub fn` name two modules of one program declare, and a consumer that
//// says which it means.
////
//// Every index that resolves an import used to be keyed by the bare symbol
//// NAME — the cross-module export index, the erlang/commonJS/beam/wasm import
//// walks that read it, the checker's registry scan, and the CLI's topological
//// sort — and a name is unique inside a module, never over a program:
//// `libs/std` declares `parse` in `json`, in `querystring` and in `url` today.
//// The `from "<mod>"` clause, the one thing that answers "which `parse`", was
//// consulted by none of them.
////
//// So `import {parse} from "one"` was answered by `two` and the defect wore
//// three faces, none of them a diagnostic: commonJS emitted
//// `require("./two.js")` and node printed `2` at exit 0; wasm linked `two`'s
//// body in and printed `2` at exit 0; erlang emitted a remote call into the
//// other module and died with `undef {two,parse,[<<"x">>]}`. Reversed — the
//// consumer naming `two` — all four REFUSED the program with `one`'s arity:
//// "'parse' expects 1 argument(s), got 2".
////
//// `two` is present and imported by nobody: that is the whole collision. A
//// second module importing `two`'s `parse` as well is NOT written here, and
//// deliberately — wasm links every module of a program into one flat
//// namespace, so two `parse` definitions in one wasm module collide there for
//// a reason that is wasm's and not this index's.

pub mod one;
pub mod two;
import {parse} from "one";

pub fn main() {
    @print(parse("x"));
}
